<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahore Resolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pakistan Resolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> Resolution was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> at its session in Lahore in March 1940. It called for independent states in the Muslim-majority areas of north-western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and became the basis of the demand for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It is often called the Pakistan Resolution, and 23 March is celebrated as Pakistan Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution.jpg" alt="Lahore Resolution" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_India_Muslim_League_Working_Committee_Lahore_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Until the mid-1930s Muslim leaders sought safeguards for Muslims within a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and obtained separate electorates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a>. After elections held under that Act, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> formed governments in six of eight provinces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>'s propaganda in 1937 to 1939 attacked those ministries and their alleged attacks on Muslim culture. By 1938 and 1939 the idea of separation was gaining ground. The Sindh Provincial Muslim League Conference, meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> in October 1938, asked the All-India Muslim League to devise a scheme under which Muslims might attain full independence.</p><p>On 9 March 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> wrote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> weekly Time and Tide that democratic systems based on homogeneous nations were not applicable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In his view there were in India two nations that both had to share the governance of their common motherland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The League met in Iqbal Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, from 22 to 24 March 1940. A nine-member subcommittee prepared the resolution, and it was moved by A. K. Fazlul Huq, the Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and approved on 23 March. It rejected the federation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a> as unworkable and unacceptable to Muslim India. Its central clause held that areas in which Muslims were numerically in a majority, in the north-western and eastern zones of India, should be grouped to constitute independent states whose constituent units would be autonomous and sovereign.</p><p>It also required safeguards for minorities in those regions and for Muslim minorities elsewhere, and it authorised the Working Committee to frame a constitutional scheme. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, proposed by Choudhary Rahmat Ali in his Pakistan Declaration of 1933, came into wide use only after the resolution. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> press and leaders were quick to describe it as a demand for Pakistan, and the name Pakistan Resolution followed soon after.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Debate continues over whether the phrase independent states in the plural meant two sovereign states. Abul Hashim of the Bengal Provincial Muslim League read it as a demand for two countries. In April 1940 the All India Azad Muslim Conference met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, with 1,400 nationalist Muslim delegates, to voice support for an independent and united India. The Muslim League worked to silence nationalist Muslims who opposed partition, often by intimidation and coercion.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> assembly was the first British Indian legislature to pass a resolution in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. The monument Minar-e-Pakistan, 60 metres tall, was built on the site in Iqbal Park. Pakistan observes 23 March as Pakistan Day, a national holiday that commemorates both the Lahore Resolution of 1940 and the republic of 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahore Resolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Muslim League, having adopted the <b>Lahore Resolution</b> of 1940 calling for independent Muslim states, won most Muslim seats in the elections of 1945–1946.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sir Syed Ahmed Khan's two-nation theory led to the All-India Muslim League in 1906, and under Muhammad Ali Jinnah the <b>Lahore Resolution</b> of 1940 demanded a separate state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1930 the poet Muhammad Iqbal proposed a consolidated Muslim state in north-western India, and in the <b>Lahore Resolution</b> of March 1940 the League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah demanded “independent states” for the Muslim-majority areas, holding that Hindus and Muslims were two nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The demand for a separate homeland for Muslims, adopted by the All-India Muslim League in the <b>Lahore Resolution</b> of 1940, led to the partition of the British Raj.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li></ul></section>
